# Goal 11: Fix remainder — wrong result for tiny subnormal inputs

## Function
`remainder` (double precision, aliased from `remainderd`)

## Source Files
- C source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/libm/mathd/remainderd.c`
- Rust source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/newexp/rust-s6/src/mathd.rs` (line ~3709)

## Problem
MISMATCH — 1 test failure.

`remainder(1e-320, 5e-324)` returns a nonzero value instead of zero.

Example:
- C:    `remainder 1e-320 5e-324 = 0x0p+0`
- Rust: `remainder 1e-320 5e-324 = 0x0.00000000007d8p-1022`

Both inputs are subnormal. The quotient is ~200, so the remainder should be exactly 0 (or very close). The Rust version has accumulated error in the subnormal reduction loop.

## What Needs to Change
Compare the subnormal handling in the Rust `remainderd` against the C `remainderd.c`. The iterative reduction for large quotients likely has a precision issue when both operands are subnormal. Check the loop that subtracts multiples of y from x.

## Success Criteria
- `remainder(1e-320, 5e-324)` returns exactly `0x0p+0`.
- Bitwise-exact match with C output for all remainder test cases.
